Did he use playing methods which are only available within a normal,
non-wizard, non-explore game that any other player could use?

If the answer is "yes", then there's no "getting away with" to be done;
this version of the game permits you to do it, so it's legal, and the
high score is a legal one and should stand.

I expect the bug to be patched in a future version of the game, and at
that point the high score list should be reset, as it is no longer
indicative of high scores for the current version of the game.  And the
game will prevent him from performing those previously mentioned
methods, and that, too, will be legal, and all will be well.

If your contention, on the other hand, is that such a thing SHOULDN'T
exist in the game, then I'm sure we all agree with you.  But as long as
it's in the game and everyone is playing on the same footing (ie has the
same access to the thing, given the same set of knowledge art had), then
it's neither "cheap" nor something to be "gotten away" with.

